        <p>I'm pleased to introduce <a href="https://one37.net/daybreak/">Daybreak</a>, a tool to neatly and regularly export your Apple Health data to files in a folder of your choosing.</p>
<p>Since the birth of our son in 2021, I've been on a journey to improve my health. It started with running. Which led to reducing alcohol. Which led to improving my diet. Which led to more regular journaling and meditation. Which has led to building a weight training habit.</p>
<p>In the process, I've lost about 75 lbs/34 kg. I feel so much better. I'm a more active parent. And, on the running front, I'm hitting a lot of fun new performance milestones.</p>
<p>As part of it all, I've been keen to lean into AI for analysis, both online and using local LLMs. But I'm not keen to upload all of my health data into a platform like ChatGPT or provide unfettered, ongoing access to the full breadth of Apple Health.</p>
<p>So, in late 2025, I built a basic app called LifeiOS to sync a particular subset of my Health data (diet, exercise, weight, etc.) to a folder. I married this with some scheduled tasks to analyse my trends and help me along the journey.</p>
<p>It's been incredibly useful.</p>
<p>As I developed <a href="https://one37.net/2026/11/introducing-breakfast.html">Breakfast</a>, I brought the two apps' aesthetics together and renamed LifeiOS to Daybreak several months ago. The two have been occupying adjacent spaces on my home screen for quite some time and form a really useful, symbiotic relationship in my journey to improve my health.</p>
<p>Daybreak is a simple utility that allows you to selectively sync daily Apple Health records to a folder of your choosing. It exports clean JSON and CSV files, allowing you to analyse and use the data as you see fit. Daybreak handles the export. You choose what happens to the files afterwards.</p>
<p>The core functionality:</p>
<ul>
<li>Health data: Choose from up to 80 daily metrics across Fitness, Nutrition, Sleep, Body measurements, Heart &amp; vitals, Mindfulness, Mobility, and Alcohol. You can select whole groups or individual metrics within them.</li>
<li>Readable files: Export daily summaries as JSON and CSV, alongside detailed workout, nutrition, and sleep records in JSON.</li>
<li>Your own folder: Keep the files on your device or in iCloud Drive (or any other service that syncs with Files), ready for spreadsheets, scripts, or whichever analysis tools you choose.</li>
<li>Historical data: Export your existing Health history and keep it current. If you decide to enable additional metrics later, you can add them to your export history too.</li>
<li>A regular routine: Sync manually, set a reminder, or use Shortcuts for automation. It's a simple tool to run once a day (or every few days) to maintain a current Health snapshot.</li>
</ul>
<p>Today's 1.0.1 update expands the available metrics to include height, waist circumference, and walking and mobility measurements. It also introduces the grouped settings and improves layouts, particularly on iPhone Duo and iPad.</p>
<p>Daybreak is now <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/daybreak-health-export/id6833011805">available in the App Store</a> for $1/£1. There are no further in-app purchases or subscriptions required. And, like Breakfast, there's no telemetry and no account required.</p>
<p>And, while I'm here, <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/breakfast-running-performance/id6790165362">Breakfast 1.0.4</a> is out today too. It adds personal bests for ultra distances up to 100 miles, new running totals widgets, an overhauled Goals section (which looks great on the Duo!), and improved sharing, including landscape images.</p>

      